<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmose I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1550–1525 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmose I was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a> around 1550 BCE. He completed the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and reunited the country. His reign marks the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, the period of the Egyptian Empire's greatest expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmose-i.jpg" alt="Ahmose I" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_Ahmose_I_MET_DP140854.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmose belonged to the Theban royal house of the Seventeenth Dynasty, a son of the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seqenenre-tao">Seqenenre Tao</a> and a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamose">Kamose</a>, the last pharaoh of that dynasty. His parents, Seqenenre Tao and Ahhotep I, were brother and sister, and the children of Senakhtenre Ahmose and Tetisheri. Thebes had rebelled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>, the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, in the time of his father or grandfather. His father was killed when Ahmose was seven, and his mummy has many wounds, which suggests that he may have died in battle. Kamose died after a reign of only three years, apparently when Ahmose was about ten.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ahmose took the throne as a boy of about ten and ruled about 25 years, dying at around 35. His mother Ahhotep acted as regent until he came of age, and descriptions of her roles, including the honorific carer for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, suggest that she consolidated the Theban power base. The conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apepi">Apepi</a> had lasted almost thirty years in all. Ahmose began the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> in about the eleventh year of the Hyksos ruler Khamudi's reign, although the order of events is not universally agreed. The main evidence is a short military diary on the back of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus">Rhind Mathematical Papyrus</a>. He took the Hyksos capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> and started raids into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> in pursuit.</p><p>He then reorganised the administration, reopened quarries, mines and trade routes, and began building on a scale not seen since the Middle Kingdom. His chief treasurer Neferperet reopened the quarries at el-Ma'asara in Ahmose's twenty-second year. The priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>, writing in the third century BCE, thought the final expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> important enough to begin a new dynasty. Ahmose's chief wife was his sister Ahmose-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ahmose's pyramid at Abydos was found in 1899 and identified as his in 1902. It was the last pyramid built by native Egyptian rulers, and its slope was about 60 degrees. Two temples built by Ahmose-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a> stand beside it. Along the same axis lay a pyramid dedicated to his grandmother Tetisheri, a rock-cut underground complex and a terraced temple against the cliffs. His eldest surviving son Amenhotep I succeeded him, as the heir Ahmose-ankh died before his father. His reign prepared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, in which Egyptian power reached its peak.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmose I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ahmose I</b>, founding pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty, completed this expulsion around 1550 BCE, reunited the country, reconquered Nubian territories lost during the preceding fragmentation, and began rebuilding the administrative and military institutions that would sustain the empire for nearly five centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ahmose I</b>, who came to the throne around 1550 BCE, captured Avaris some years later and then besieged Sharuhen in southern Canaan, driving out the Hyksos and founding the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/fifteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Fifteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ahmose I</b>, probably Kamose's younger brother, came to the throne around 1550 BCE and completed the war, later taking Avaris and reuniting Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/seventeenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Seventeenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
